# ROM-only mapping: the generated boot ROM expanded to sky130 standard cells.
#
#   python3 scripts/fw_build/gen_rom_rtl.py build/fw/boot_rom.hex build/rom/boot_rom_soc.v
#   yosys -s asic/synthesis/rom_map.ys
#
# Why a separate flow
# -------------------
# scripts/fw_build/gen_rom_rtl.py bakes the image into a case statement. Yosys
# infers that case as a memory, so it must be expanded with `memory_map` before
# techmap, otherwise the "ROM" is still a $mem_v2 leaf with a case-shaped name
# and nothing has actually been synthesised.
#
# Expanding it inside the full-SoC flow would also expand the TCM/SRAM arrays
# that the macro wrappers deliberately keep as blackbox macros, which is neither
# wanted nor affordable. Measuring the ROM on its own is therefore the honest
# unit: this is the cell count and the mapping cost of the ROM decision, not of
# the SoC.
#
# The result is a correctness demonstration and an AREA measurement. It is NOT
# a characterised ASIC ROM: a mux tree has no bit-cell array, no precharge, no
# sense amps and no ROM-specific timing/power model. See
# reports/ROM_QUALIFICATION.md.

read_verilog -sv build/rom/boot_rom_soc.v
hierarchy -check -top boot_rom
proc
memory_collect
memory_map
opt_clean

read_liberty -lib build/asic_evidence/sky130_full_subset.lib
techmap -map +/techmap.v
techmap -map build/asic_evidence/sky130_map.v
techmap -map build/asic_evidence/sky130_map.v
hierarchy -check
opt_clean

tee -o build/asic_evidence/structural_check/boot_rom_cells.json stat -top boot_rom -json
check -assert
